        <!-- SECTION 4: Matching a Sealer to Your Wood -->
        <div id="wood-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Matching a Sealer to Your Wood
          </h2>

          <p>
            Different woods absorb and weather differently, and the right product varies more than most guides admit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pressure-Treated Pine</h3>
          <p>
            The most common decking material and the most demanding. New pressure-treated lumber is often still wet with treatment chemicals and will not accept a sealer — wait until a sprinkle of water soaks in within a minute or two rather than beading, which can take anywhere from a few weeks to six months depending on how it was stored. Pressure-treated pine also moves a great deal seasonally, which favors penetrating oil-based products that flex with the wood over film-formers that crack.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cedar and Redwood</h3>
          <p>
            Naturally rot-resistant but very prone to UV graying, and both are soft enough that pressure washing damages them easily. These woods benefit most from semi-transparent pigmented sealers, which preserve the color that is the whole reason to buy them. Cedar in particular can bleed tannins, so use a product designed to handle extractive bleed or expect some discoloration in the first season.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tropical Hardwoods (Ipe, Cumaru, Teak)</h3>
          <p>
            Dense and naturally oily, which means most sealers cannot penetrate and will sit on the surface and peel. Use a product specifically formulated for dense hardwoods, expect thin coats and low coverage, and accept a shorter recoat cycle — often annually — because so little product can get in. Many owners of these decks simply let them gray naturally, which is a legitimate choice since the wood itself does not rot.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fences and Vertical Surfaces</h3>
          <p>
            Vertical wood sheds water and gets less direct sun exposure than a horizontal deck, so sealers last considerably longer — often twice as long on a fence as on a deck of the same wood with the same product. Film-forming products are also safer here, since water does not stand on a vertical surface.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Surface Preparation -->
        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Surface Preparation
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealer applied to dirty, gray, or damp wood fails early regardless of quality. Preparation is where a two-year result becomes a four-year one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clean, Then Brighten</h3>
          <p>
            Wash with a dedicated deck cleaner to remove dirt, mildew, and the failed remains of a previous finish. Then apply a wood brightener — an oxalic acid product — which neutralizes the cleaner, removes tannin and iron stains, and restores the wood's natural tone. Skipping the brightener leaves the surface alkaline, which shortens the life of the sealer that goes over it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Go Easy With a Pressure Washer</h3>
          <p>
            Pressure washing is the fastest way to damage a deck. Softwoods like cedar and pine erode under high pressure, leaving a furry surface that soaks up sealer unevenly and looks worse afterward. Keep it under 1,500 psi, use a fan tip at least 12 inches away, and let chemistry do the work rather than force.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sand Only If the Surface Is Fuzzy</h3>
          <p>
            Loose, weathered fibers prevent good penetration. A light sanding with 60-80 grit removes them. Do not sand smooth — too fine a surface closes the pores and reduces how much sealer the wood can accept.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wait for Dry Wood</h3>
          <p>
            Wood should be below roughly 15% moisture content before sealing, which typically means 48 hours of dry weather after washing, longer in humid conditions. The simple field test: sprinkle water on the surface. If it soaks in within a minute or two, the wood is ready to accept sealer. If it beads, either the wood is still wet or an old finish remains.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Application: Getting the Most From the Product -->
        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Application: Getting the Most From the Product
          </h2>

          <p>
            Exterior wood sealing rewards restraint more than effort. The most common application mistake is applying too much.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Thin Coats, and Back-Wipe the Excess</h3>
          <p>
            Penetrating sealers can only absorb so much; anything beyond that sits on the surface, stays tacky, attracts dirt, and eventually flakes. Apply until the wood stops accepting product, then wipe or brush out any excess within 15-30 minutes. Two thin coats beat one heavy one every time.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Work Board to Board, Full Length</h3>
          <p>
            Stopping in the middle of a board leaves a visible lap mark that no amount of subsequent coating hides. Plan your sections so you always finish a full board length, and work from a shaded end toward the sun if you can.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Avoid Direct Sun and Extremes</h3>
          <p>
            Hot wood flashes the carrier off before the resin penetrates, leaving product on the surface. Apply on an overcast day, or follow the shade around the deck. Most products want 50-90°F with no rain forecast for 24-48 hours.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do Not Forget End Grain</h3>
          <p>
            The cut ends of boards absorb water many times faster than face grain, and they are where rot usually starts. Flood the end grain of every board, especially on stair stringers, post bottoms, and rail ends. It uses very little product and prevents the most expensive kind of failure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Test on a Hidden Board First</h3>
          <p>
            Color varies dramatically with wood species, age, and how much previous finish remains. Apply your chosen product to an out-of-the-way board or an offcut and let it dry fully before committing to the whole deck. Wet color and dry color are frequently very different.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Maintenance and Recoating -->
        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Maintenance and Recoating
          </h2>

          <p>
            The right recoating rhythm depends entirely on whether you chose a penetrating or a film-forming product, and getting it wrong is what turns maintenance into restoration.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Water Test Tells You When</h3>
          <p>
            Sprinkle water on the deck a few times a year. While it beads, the sealer is working. When it soaks in and darkens the wood, the sealer has worn through and it is time to recoat. This is a far better indicator than a calendar, since exposure varies enormously between a shaded north side and a south-facing deck.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Recoat Penetrating Sealers Before They Fail</h3>
          <p>
            Penetrating oils wear away rather than peeling, so recoating is simply cleaning and reapplying — no stripping. Doing it a little early is easy and cheap. Letting it go until the wood grays means cleaning, brightening, possibly sanding, and a much longer weekend.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Film-Formers Eventually Need Stripping</h3>
          <p>
            Water-based film-forming products and solid stains build up with each coat, and once they start peeling the only real fix is stripping or sanding back to bare wood. Plan for this — it arrives eventually with any film product on a horizontal surface, and it is a genuinely large job.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep It Clean Between Coats</h3>
          <p>
            Sweep regularly, keep planters on feet rather than sitting directly on boards, and clear leaves out of the gaps between deck boards where they trap moisture. Trim back vegetation that blocks airflow. These small habits routinely add a year to a finish's life.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do Not Mix Chemistries</h3>
          <p>
            Oil-based sealers do not adhere well over water-based films, and vice versa. If you are switching product types, strip back to bare wood first. Applying one over the other is the most common cause of a finish that fails in a single season for no apparent reason.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best exterior wood sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                TWP 1500 Series was the best performer in our two-year test, still beading water at 22 months with excellent color retention and no peeling. For a first-time applicator, Ready Seal is nearly as durable and dramatically more forgiving to apply. For water-based, DEFY Extreme's zinc UV blockers deliver the best color retention in that category.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does exterior wood sealer last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Clear sealers last 6-12 months before UV graying begins. Semi-transparent products last 2-3 years, semi-solid 3-5 years, and solid stains 4-7 years. Vertical surfaces like fences last roughly twice as long as horizontal decks using the same product, because water does not stand and sun exposure is less direct.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is clear or tinted wood sealer better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tinted, in nearly every case. UV light is what breaks down wood surface fibers and causes graying, and blocking UV requires pigment — no clear resin substitutes for it. In our testing, every clear sealer began graying within 7-11 months while semi-transparent products lasted two to three times longer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When can you seal new pressure-treated wood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                When it will accept the sealer, which can be anywhere from a few weeks to six months after installation depending on how wet the lumber was and how it has been stored. Test by sprinkling water on the surface: if it soaks in within a minute or two, the wood is ready. If it beads, wait longer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you apply wood sealer over an old finish?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if it is the same type of product and still in sound condition. Penetrating oils recoat cleanly onto themselves after washing. Film-forming products that are peeling must be stripped or sanded back to bare wood. Never apply oil-based over water-based or vice versa — poor adhesion is the most common cause of a finish that fails in one season.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know when to reseal my deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sprinkle water on the surface a few times a year. While it beads, the sealer is working. When it soaks in and darkens the wood, it is time to recoat. This is far more reliable than a calendar, since a shaded north side and a south-facing deck wear at completely different rates.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I pressure wash my deck before sealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With caution. Pressure washing is the fastest way to damage a softwood deck, eroding fibers and leaving a fuzzy surface that absorbs sealer unevenly. Stay under 1,500 psi with a fan tip at least 12 inches away, and rely on a deck cleaner and a brightener to do the actual work rather than pressure.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Mistakes That Ruin an Exterior Wood Finish
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every premature sealer failure comes down to one of these five things.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing Clear Because You Want the Wood to Show</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Clear sealers cannot block UV, and UV is what grays and fuzzes the surface within a year. If natural wood appearance matters, use a lightly tinted toner rather than a true clear, and accept an annual recoat. Otherwise you are protecting against water while the sun destroys the surface anyway.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying Too Much Product</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Penetrating sealers absorb only so much; anything beyond that sits on top, stays tacky, collects dirt, and flakes. Apply until the wood stops taking it, then wipe out the excess within 15-30 minutes. Two thin coats always outperform one heavy one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Wood That Is Still Wet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer applied over moisture traps it in the wood and prevents proper penetration, producing a finish that fails within a season. Wait 48 hours of dry weather after washing, longer in humid conditions, and test by sprinkling water — it should soak in within a minute or two.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressure Washing Too Aggressively</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                High pressure erodes softwood fibers, leaving a furry surface that drinks sealer unevenly and looks worse afterward than before you started. Stay under 1,500 psi, keep the tip 12 inches back, and let a deck cleaner and brightener do the work.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing Oil-Based and Water-Based Products</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Oil does not adhere reliably over a water-based film, and water-based does not bond well over oil. Switching chemistries requires stripping to bare wood first. Applying one over the other is the classic cause of a finish that inexplicably fails after a single season.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

